### What is the fork-join or scatter-gather pattern?

The execution flow has four phases: split, fork, join, and combine. The parent divides the work into genuinely independent units, runs them concurrently, waits for all to finish, and merges the results. Independence is essential: if child tasks depend on each other you cannot run them concurrently.

```java
package com.bank.portfolio;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class PortfolioService {

    private final AccountService accountService;
    private final EquityService equityService;
    private final MutualFundService fundService;
    private final ExecutorService executor;

    public PortfolioService(AccountService accountService,
                             EquityService equityService,
                             MutualFundService fundService) {
        this.accountService = accountService;
        this.equityService = equityService;
        this.fundService = fundService;
        // Dedicated bounded executor for I/O calls
        this.executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);
    }

    public BigDecimal getNetWorth(String customerId) throws Exception {
        // FORK: submit all three children concurrently
        CompletableFuture<BigDecimal> cashF =
            CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
                () -> accountService.getTotalBalance(customerId),
                executor);

        CompletableFuture<BigDecimal> equityF =
            CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
                () -> equityService.getHoldingsValue(customerId),
                executor)
            .exceptionally(ex -> BigDecimal.ZERO); // Fallback

        CompletableFuture<BigDecimal> fundF =
            CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
                () -> fundService.getHoldingsValue(customerId),
                executor)
            .exceptionally(ex -> BigDecimal.ZERO); // Fallback

        // JOIN: wait for all with timeout
        CompletableFuture.allOf(cashF, equityF, fundF)
            .get(2, TimeUnit.SECONDS);

        // COMBINE: merge results
        BigDecimal cash = cashF.join();
        BigDecimal equity = equityF.join();
        BigDecimal fund = fundF.join();

        return cash.add(equity).add(fund);
    }
}
```

```text
Sequential execution:
  Cash:   200ms
  Equity: 300ms
  Fund:   150ms
  Total:  650ms

Parallel execution (scatter-gather):
  Cash:   200ms ─┐
  Equity: 300ms ─┤ all run concurrently
  Fund:   150ms ─┘
  Total:  300ms (slowest child)

Speedup: 2.2x
```

### How does the fork phase work?

Each child is submitted to a thread pool with `supplyAsync`, which returns immediately with a `CompletableFuture`. The actual work runs on an executor thread. After creating three futures, all three are running in parallel.

```java
package com.bank.portfolio;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;

public class ForkPhaseExample {

    public void demonstrate(String customerId,
                             ExecutorService executor) {
        // Each supplyAsync returns IMMEDIATELY
        // The lambda runs on an executor thread
        CompletableFuture<BigDecimal> f1 =
            CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
                () -> fetchBalance(customerId), executor);

        CompletableFuture<BigDecimal> f2 =
            CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
                () -> fetchEquity(customerId), executor);

        // At this point, BOTH are running concurrently
        // on executor threads
        System.out.println("Both tasks submitted, running in parallel");
    }

    private BigDecimal fetchBalance(String id) {
        // Simulates I/O call to account service
        return new BigDecimal("150000.00");
    }

    private BigDecimal fetchEquity(String id) {
        // Simulates I/O call to equity service
        return new BigDecimal("250000.00");
    }
}
```

### How does the join phase work?

The parent waits for every child with `allOf`, which returns a future that completes only when all children complete. Always bound with a timeout so one slow dependency cannot hang the request.

```java
package com.bank.portfolio;

import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeoutException;

public class JoinPhaseExample {

    public void demonstrate(CompletableFuture<?>... children)
            throws Exception {
        try {
            // allOf completes when ALL children complete
            // get() with timeout bounds the wait
            CompletableFuture.allOf(children)
                .get(2, TimeUnit.SECONDS);

            System.out.println("All children completed within 2s");
        } catch (TimeoutException e) {
            System.out.println("At least one child timed out!");
            // Cancel remaining children
            for (CompletableFuture<?> child : children) {
                child.cancel(true);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Join behavior:
  Child 1: completes in 200ms
  Child 2: completes in 300ms
  Child 3: completes in 150ms
  allOf: completes at 300ms (slowest)

  With timeout:
  Child 1: completes in 200ms
  Child 2: hangs (service down)
  Timeout at 2s -> TimeoutException
  Action: use fallback for child 2
```

### How does error handling work?

If any child fails, `allOf` completes exceptionally. Attach `exceptionally` to each child for per-child fallbacks so one failure does not crash the whole aggregate.

```java
package com.bank.portfolio;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;

public class ErrorHandlingExample {

    public BigDecimal getNetWorthGraceful(String customerId,
                                           ExecutorService executor) {
        CompletableFuture<BigDecimal> cashF =
            CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
                () -> accountService.getTotalBalance(customerId),
                executor);
            // No fallback: cash is critical, fail the whole thing

        CompletableFuture<BigDecimal> equityF =
            CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
                () -> equityService.getHoldingsValue(customerId),
                executor)
            .exceptionally(ex -> {
                // Fallback: equity service down -> show 0
                System.out.println("Equity service down: " +
                    ex.getMessage());
                return BigDecimal.ZERO;
            });

        CompletableFuture<BigDecimal> fundF =
            CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
                () -> fundService.getHoldingsValue(customerId),
                executor)
            .exceptionally(ex -> BigDecimal.ZERO);

        return cashF.thenCombine(equityF, BigDecimal::add)
                    .thenCombine(fundF, BigDecimal::add)
                    .join();
    }
}
```

```text
Error handling strategies:
  Cash (critical):   no fallback -> fail the whole dashboard
  Equity (optional): fallback to 0 -> show partial net worth
  Fund (optional):   fallback to 0 -> show partial net worth

  Equity service down:
  Net worth = 150000 (cash) + 0 (equity fallback) + 75000 (fund)
  Dashboard shows: "Net Worth: 225000 (equity data unavailable)"
```

### Why use a dedicated executor?

The common `ForkJoinPool` is sized for CPU-bound work. Blocking I/O calls on it can starve the entire application. Use a dedicated bounded executor as a bulkhead.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

@Configuration
public class ExecutorConfig {

    @Bean(name = "portfolioExecutor")
    public ExecutorService portfolioExecutor() {
        // Dedicated pool for portfolio I/O calls
        // Does not starve the shared ForkJoinPool
        return Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);
    }
}
```

```text
WRONG: use ForkJoinPool.commonPool()
  Shared by all CompletableFuture.supplyAsync() without executor
  I/O calls block these threads -> stream().parallel() starves
  Other features starve

CORRECT: dedicated bounded executor
  Portfolio I/O: 10 threads (bulkhead)
  Other features: unaffected
```

### How does thenCombine provide a cleaner composition?

Instead of `allOf` + `join()` on each, `thenCombine` chains the combination functionally.

```java
package com.bank.portfolio;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;

public class FunctionalComposition {

    public CompletableFuture<BigDecimal> getNetWorthAsync(
            String customerId, ExecutorService executor) {
        CompletableFuture<BigDecimal> cashF =
            CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
                () -> accountService.getTotalBalance(customerId),
                executor);
        CompletableFuture<BigDecimal> equityF =
            CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
                () -> equityService.getHoldingsValue(customerId),
                executor)
            .exceptionally(ex -> BigDecimal.ZERO);
        CompletableFuture<BigDecimal> fundF =
            CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
                () -> fundService.getHoldingsValue(customerId),
                executor)
            .exceptionally(ex -> BigDecimal.ZERO);

        // Functional composition: no manual join needed
        return cashF
            .thenCombine(equityF, BigDecimal::add)
            .thenCombine(fundF, BigDecimal::add);
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is using the shared ForkJoinPool for blocking I/O. Another is not setting a timeout on the join, which lets one slow child hang the entire request.

```text
WRONG: no timeout
  Child 3 hangs forever -> dashboard never loads
  Rajesh stares at spinner indefinitely

CORRECT: 2-second timeout
  Child 3 hangs -> timeout after 2s -> fallback returns 0
  Dashboard loads with partial data in 2s
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer propagates correlation IDs into child tasks for distributed tracing, sizes the dedicated executor based on the number of downstream services and their expected latency, and distinguishes between critical children (whose failure fails the aggregate) and optional children (whose failure degrades gracefully). In reactive stacks, the same pattern uses `Mono.zip` instead of `CompletableFuture.allOf`.

```java
package com.bank.portfolio;

public class SeniorGuidelines {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Senior guidelines for scatter-gather:");
        System.out.println("  1. Propagate traceId and paymentId to child threads");
        System.out.println("  2. Size executor: (child count * expected concurrency)");
        System.out.println("  3. Classify children: critical (fail whole) vs optional (fallback)");
        System.out.println("  4. Always set a timeout on the join (allOf.get(2, SECONDS))");
        System.out.println("  5. Never use ForkJoinPool.commonPool() for blocking I/O");
        System.out.println("  6. In reactive: Mono.zip() replaces allOf for non-blocking");
        System.out.println("  7. Monitor: per-child latency, fallback rate, timeout rate");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Senior guidelines for scatter-gather:
  1. Propagate traceId and paymentId to child threads
  2. Size executor: (child count * expected concurrency)
  3. Classify children: critical (fail whole) vs optional (fallback)
  4. Always set a timeout on the join (allOf.get(2, SECONDS))
  5. Never use ForkJoinPool.commonPool() for blocking I/O
  6. In reactive: Mono.zip() replaces allOf for non-blocking
  7. Monitor: per-child latency, fallback rate, timeout rate
```
